ing  reflected in russia with dw  correspondent killian bayer he's part of  our moscow bureau which is currently  operating from latvia's capital riga  welcome to the studio killian how's  russian media reporting these losses  taking place in ukraine  well i think it's clear it's a dramatic  defeat for the russian military and  especially kupiansk and isiam were  strategic railway hubs  and we saw the first reports  for russians really come in through  pro-russian war bloggers on telegram  channels there was quite some harsh  criticism saying the russian army has  abandoned us um it kind of shifted then  to state television we heard and some of  the the the talk shows we heard um  people saying you know oh  there's some bad stuff happening in  ukraine we should all get together and  pray for our soldiers who are fighting  hordes of ukrainian soldiers equipped  with western weapons um  one of the the top talk shows on prime  time you know i think the word don't  panic was  was said about 10 times which kind of  had the opposite effect i kind of felt  now the narrative has shifted and the  ministry of defense put out the  statement that there's a regrouping  happening and that seems to be kind of  the the way things are are being  portrayed but still  there is some a very surprising  outspoken criticism of the way that  russia is conducting the war um also  something that i  found quite surprising is that the word  war is falling more and more often on um  on  national television in russia and this  could of course be a slip of the tongue  because people are are nervous or  surprised by what's happening but maybe  it's also deliberate and uh maybe you  know  the russian public is being prepared for  a mobilization um but it's it's really  hard to say that's just speculation okay  because of course prior to this this has  been described as a special military  operation and and and this  this regrouping as russian local uh  elections have just taken place  has the war b